    "title": {
     "en": "A short story"
    },
    "text": {
     "en": "Before a cricket match the captains toss a coin. Nobody knows the result, yet we can say the chance of heads is one out of two."
    }
   },
   {
    "id": "explain",
    "type": "explanation",
    "provenance": "original",
    "keepEnglishTerms": [
     "outcome",
     "event",
     "favourable outcomes",
     "sure event"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "An experiment is an activity with results that we cannot predict, like tossing a coin or rolling a die. Each possible result is an outcome and a group of outcomes is an event.\nThe probability of an event E is P(E) = number of favourable outcomes ÷ total number of outcomes, when all outcomes are equally likely. For a die, P(getting 4) = 1/6.\nProbability is always between 0 and 1. An impossible event has probability 0 and a sure event has probability 1. For example P(getting 7 on a die) = 0.\nThe probability that an event does not happen is 1 − P(E). If P(rain) = 2/5 then P(no rain) = 3/5. The probabilities of all outcomes add up to 1."
    }
   },
